+## E-22. Stored Content Is Never Regenerated by the AI; Additions Are Appended `[ACTIVE]` 🔴 CRITICAL
+
+*Added: v1.8.0, November 2026 -- learned on IBL Lesson Planner (Sprint 05, Sprint 06)*
+
+AI output that ADDS to stored content (a rubric, a video, a new version of a section) is appended to the stored text deterministically, word for word, as numbered amendments; the original stays byte for byte. The AI is never asked to regenerate a stored document "including" the addition. If replacing stored content is unavoidable, the result is checked for completeness against what it replaces (every section still there, not much shorter), shown to the user before it is saved, and the replaced text is kept for a one-click restore. A "success" message is shown only after the visible result is checked.
+
+**Learned from:** IBL Lesson Planner — "Ugradi u plan" asked the AI to rewrite a saved lesson plan with the conversation's suggestions: once it dropped the whole UDICA section, once it reduced a requested rubric to one sentence (Director catches, Sprint 06). Earlier a section rewrite reported success on an empty card (Sprint 05). Numbered amendments ("AMANDMAN 1, 2 …") fixed it; a test checks every original line is still there, in order.
+
+---
+
+## E-23. Zero-Tolerance Output Rules Are Enforced After Generation `[ACTIVE]` 🟡 STANDARD
+
+*Added: v1.8.0, November 2026 -- learned on IBL Lesson Planner (Sprint 05, Sprint 06)*
+
+A prompt instruction reduces a failure; only a deterministic layer after generation guarantees it is gone. When an output rule allows no exception (a language's template lines, a forbidden character, Markdown leftovers), apply it in one display/export layer used by every surface, and measure it on what the user actually sees.
+
+**Learned from:** IBL Lesson Planner — prompt amendments cut German plans copying Bosnian template lines from 3/13 to 1/7, never to 0 (Sprint 05); rule lines, Markdown and spaced dashes reached the screen (Sprint 06). One display layer (`display.ts`) reached 0.
+
+---
+
+## E-24. Lazy-Loaded SPAs Survive a Release While Open `[ACTIVE]` 🟡 STANDARD
+
+*Added: v1.8.0, November 2026 -- learned on IBL Lesson Planner (Sprint 06)*
+
+Users keep tabs open across releases. A page that loads code on demand reloads once (rate-limited) on the bundler's chunk-load error event (`vite:preloadError`); the SPA catch-all rewrite never covers `/assets/*`, so a missing chunk is a clean 404, not HTML; and the opened route's chunks are preloaded from `index.html`, so on-demand pages add no round trip.
+
+**Learned from:** IBL Lesson Planner — after a merge, "Word dokument" in an open tab asked for the previous release's chunk and got `index.html` ("MIME type text/html"); the export failed until Ctrl+F5.
+
+---
+
+## E-25. Code Shared by Two Runtimes; Every CI Step Runs Locally Before Push `[ACTIVE]` 🟡 STANDARD
+
+*Added: v1.8.0, November 2026 -- learned on IBL Lesson Planner (Sprint 05, Sprint 06)*
+
+A source file imported by two runtimes (Vite and Deno, browser and server) uses runtime-neutral imports: relative paths with explicit extensions, no bundler aliases. Before a push, run every command of the CI workflow locally, including the second runtime's checks (e.g. `npx -y deno@2 check|test`), not only the unit tests and the type check.
+
+**Learned from:** IBL Lesson Planner — a `@/` alias added to a file that the Deno eval script also imports failed `deno check` in CI (PR #48); a cloud worker restart had removed Deno and tests ran through a shim (Sprint 05).
+
+---
